    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-23367.s12">
        <fig id="article-23367.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Right Hip and Femoral Muscles, Anterior View. This illustration shows the tensor fasciae latae, thoracic vertebrae, quadratus lumborum, psoas minor and major, crest of ilium, anterior superior iliac spine, iliacus, sartorius, pectineus, adductor longus, gracilis, adductor magnus, rectus femoris, vastus lateralis and medialis, tibia, patella, and quadriceps tendon. Henry Vandyke Carter, Public Domain, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray430" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-23367.s13">
        <fig id="article-23367.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Superficial and Deep Structures of the Femoral Region. The diagram provides a comprehensive look at the neurovascular bundles, including the femoral nerve (yellow), femoral artery (red), and femoral vein (blue). The anatomical relationship between these structures is detailed as they transition from the inguinal region down toward the patella. Johannes Sobotta, MD, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=View+of+anterior+thigh&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="800px-Anterior_view_of_the_thigh" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
